    ]
  },
  "verdict": "Choose an online bank if you rarely deposit cash, are comfortable without a branch, and want the higher savings yields and lower fees online banks often compete on. Choose a traditional bank if you deposit cash regularly, want in-person service, or want checking, savings, and other products under one roof. If you want both, keep one of each: cash and in-person needs at the traditional bank, savings at the online bank. This comparison is also not for anyone choosing a credit union, because the National Credit Union Administration (NCUA) insures credit unions, and our Credit Union vs Bank guide handles that choice. Our verdict would tilt toward a traditional bank if its current savings yield and fees match an online bank's terms, and toward an online bank if it offers a cash deposit method you can use easily. A bank that is not FDIC-insured fails this comparison at either type, because its deposits carry no FDIC coverage.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Traditional Bank vs Online Bank by How You Handle Money",
      "content": "An online bank fits someone who rarely handles cash and wants lower fees and higher savings yields, and a traditional bank fits someone who deposits cash or wants help in a branch. Both of those are tendencies that vary by institution. The current terms at a specific bank can break the pattern, so compare banks by name.\n\nStart with cash. Online banks have no branches or only a few, while traditional banks offer branches and tend to make cash deposits simpler. \n\nThen weigh yield and fees. Online banks often compete on higher savings yields and lower fees. If you keep a large savings balance and rarely need a teller, an online bank deserves a close look.\n\nDeposit insurance works the same way at both types. It depends on whether the specific bank is FDIC-insured, and a bank's branch count does not change that."
    },
    {
      "heading": "FDIC Insurance for a Traditional Bank vs Online Bank",
      "content": "The FDIC insures deposits to at least $250,000 per depositor, per ownership category, at each FDIC-insured bank, whether that bank runs branches or operates online. Coverage is automatic for any deposit account opened at an FDIC-insured bank. You do not apply for it separately.\n\nThe condition is the word insured. An online bank's deposits are covered only if that online bank is FDIC-insured, and the same rule holds for a bank with a branch on your street. Search the FDIC's [BankFind tool](https://banks.data.fdic.gov/bankfind-suite/bankfind) by bank name, FDIC certificate number, web address, location, or branch before you deposit anything.\n\nThe limit applies at each insured bank, which changes the math for larger balances. A depositor with accounts in the same ownership category at two separate FDIC-insured banks, one traditional and one online, has at least $250,000 of coverage at each bank, or at least $500,000 across the two. For how ownership categories are counted, read the FDIC's [deposit insurance FAQ](https://www.fdic.gov/resources/deposit-insurance/faq).\n\nThe FDIC's phone line for deposit insurance questions is 1-877-ASK-FDIC. Use it if you have deposit insurance questions."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Branches, In-Person Service, and Cash Deposits",
      "content": "Traditional banks offer branches and in-person services, and they tend to make cash deposits simpler than online banks do. If paper money passes through your hands often, a branch gives you a place to deposit it. \n\nOnline banks have no branches or only a few. How a given online bank takes cash deposits and hands out cash varies by institution. Ask any online bank how cash gets into and out of the account before you route income to it.\n\nStart with a quick count. Write down how many times you deposited paper bills or coins last month, and how often you walked into a branch for any reason. If both numbers are zero, the higher yields and lower fees online banks often compete on deserve more weight.\n\nIf either number shows up every month, put a traditional bank first. In-person service also covers more than cash. If you want in-person service, traditional banks tend to offer it."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Savings Yields and Monthly Fees",
      "content": "Online banks often compete on higher savings yields and lower fees, but current terms vary by institution at both types. No single yield or fee figure describes either one. Compare current terms at the specific banks you are considering.\n\nTo turn a yield difference into dollars, run your balance through our [high-yield savings calculator](/investing/high-yield-savings-calculator/) once with each bank's current rate. Our roundup of the [best high-yield savings accounts](/roundup/best-high-yield-savings-accounts/) gives you accounts to put side by side.\n\nAdd any monthly fee to our [monthly budget calculator](/budget/monthly-budget-calculator/) next to your other bills before you decide. If you are weighing a high-yield savings account (HYSA) against a certificate of deposit (CD) or a money market account, read [HYSA vs CD](/compare/hysa-vs-cd/) and [HYSA vs Money Market](/compare/hysa-vs-money-market/)."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Account Opening, Apps, and Customer Support",
      "content": "Account opening, app features, and customer support vary by institution, so compare the specific banks you are considering on each one. \n\nA traditional bank offers in-person services. An online bank has no branches or only a few. Before opening, ask an online bank how it handles support and when you can reach a person.\n\nIf you want checking, savings, and other products under one roof, a traditional bank fits that goal. An online bank's product range varies by institution, so compare its list against the accounts you plan to use. For help splitting money between the two basic accounts, see [Checking vs Savings Account](/compare/checking-vs-savings-account/).\n\nHow you open the account does not change its insurance. Coverage is automatic for any deposit account opened at an FDIC-insured bank, including one you open from a phone."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Spotting a Fake Online Bank Site",
      "content": "The FDIC's [alert on bank impersonation scams and fake banks](https://www.fdic.gov/consumer-resource-center/2025-06/bank-impersonation-scams-and-fake-banks) warns that fake bank sites misuse the FDIC name or logo, or display \"Member FDIC\" without being insured. A badge on a website is not proof of coverage.\n\nBefore you share personal information or send money, compare the bank's official name against the web address in your browser. Look for misspellings or unusual web addresses. Then search the bank in the FDIC's [BankFind tool](https://banks.data.fdic.gov/bankfind-suite/bankfind) by name, FDIC certificate number, or web address.\n\nThese steps apply to any bank, including one with branches. For deposit insurance questions, the FDIC's phone line is 1-877-ASK-FDIC."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Keeping One Traditional Bank and One Online Bank",
      "content": "Keeping one account at each type gives you a traditional bank for cash deposits and in-person service, plus an online bank for the higher yields and lower fees it may offer. You do not have to give up branch access to chase a better savings yield.\n\nOne way to split them puts your checking account and cash deposits at the traditional bank and your savings at the online bank. Compare current terms at both before you move money, since either bank's yield or fees can beat the general pattern.\n\nWhen both banks are FDIC-insured, each one's coverage counts separately: at least $250,000 per depositor, per ownership category, at each bank. Search both in [BankFind](https://banks.data.fdic.gov/bankfind-suite/bankfind) before you open either account. The tradeoff is a second set of terms to compare."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Credit Unions and NCUA Insurance",
      "content": "Credit unions are insured by the [National Credit Union Administration (NCUA)](https://www.ncua.gov). The FDIC does not insure credit unions, so the FDIC coverage rules described above apply to banks. If a credit union is on your list next to a traditional bank and an online bank, read [Credit Union vs Bank](/compare/credit-union-vs-bank/) before you choose."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Steps to Choose a Traditional Bank vs Online Bank",
      "content": "Settle the traditional bank vs online bank choice by counting your cash use, comparing current terms at two named banks, and confirming FDIC insurance. Write down last month's cash deposits and branch visits, since those two numbers show whether branch access earns its place.\n\nPick one traditional bank and one online bank, then compare their current savings yields and monthly fees for the accounts you would open. Before you open either account, search both banks in the FDIC's [BankFind tool](https://banks.data.fdic.gov/bankfind-suite/bankfind) and make sure each official name matches its web address. Then open the account at whichever bank wins on cash access and current terms."
    }
  ],
  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "Which is safer, traditional bank or digital bank?",
      "answer": "Neither type is safer by default, because deposit protection depends on the specific bank. The FDIC insures deposits to at least $250,000 per depositor, per ownership category, at each FDIC-insured bank, and coverage is automatic for any deposit account opened there. A digital bank is covered only if it is FDIC-insured, so search it in the FDIC's [BankFind tool](https://banks.data.fdic.gov/bankfind-suite/bankfind) before you deposit."
    },
    {
      "question": "Traditional bank or online bank?",
      "answer": "Pick an online bank if you rarely handle cash and want the higher savings yields and lower fees online banks often compete on. Pick a traditional bank if you deposit cash, want branches and in-person service, or want everything under one roof. Terms vary by institution, so compare current yields and fees at the specific banks you are considering."
    },
    {
      "question": "Is Capital One a traditional bank or online bank?",
      "answer": "Whether a bank runs branches, operates online, or does both varies by institution. To confirm Capital One's FDIC insurance status, search its name or web address in the FDIC's [BankFind tool](https://banks.data.fdic.gov/bankfind-suite/bankfind)."
    },
    {
      "question": "Is my money insured at an online bank?",
      "answer": "Your money is insured at an online bank only if that bank is FDIC-insured. In that case, deposits are covered to at least $250,000 per depositor, per ownership category, and coverage is automatic for any deposit account you open. Search the bank by name, FDIC certificate number, or web address in [BankFind](https://banks.data.fdic.gov/bankfind-suite/bankfind) before depositing."
    },
    {
      "question": "How can I tell if an online bank is fake?",
      "answer": "Compare the bank's official name against its web address and look for misspellings or unusual web addresses. The FDIC's [alert on fake banks](https://www.fdic.gov/consumer-resource-center/2025-06/bank-impersonation-scams-and-fake-banks) warns that fake sites misuse the FDIC name or logo, or display \"Member FDIC\" without being insured. Search the bank in [BankFind](https://banks.data.fdic.gov/bankfind-suite/bankfind) before you share personal information or send money, or call 1-877-ASK-FDIC with deposit insurance questions."
    },
    {
      "question": "Can I keep accounts at both a traditional bank and an online bank?",
      "answer": "Yes, you can hold accounts at both, which lets you use a traditional bank for cash and in-person service and an online bank for savings. If both banks are FDIC-insured, coverage applies at each bank: at least $250,000 per depositor, per ownership category. For how ownership categories are counted, read the FDIC's [deposit insurance FAQ](https://www.fdic.gov/resources/deposit-insurance/faq)."
    },
    {
      "question": "Are credit unions covered by the FDIC?",
      "answer": "No. Credit unions are insured by the [National Credit Union Administration (NCUA)](https://www.ncua.gov), and the FDIC does not insure them. To compare a credit union against a bank, read [Credit Union vs Bank](/compare/credit-union-vs-bank/)."
    }
  ],
